WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:03.680
We live on a planet of insects.

00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:07.880
They make up around 70%
of all known species on the Earth,

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:11.760
and their combined biomass
is 16 times that of humans.

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:14.560
300 million years ago,

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:18.640
giant dragonflies
with wingspans of 75 centimetres

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:20.800
soared amongst the tree ferns.

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:24.800
Nowadays, insects
are extraordinarily diverse,

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:29.360
existing in a bewildering array
of colours, shapes and sizes,

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:32.760
and they play a crucial role
in life on Earth.

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:34.720
They're food
for a great many animals,

00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:37.600
including most birds, bats, lizards,

00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:39.520
amphibians and freshwater fish.

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:45.360
80% of the world's wild
plant species depend on insects

00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:46.520
to pollinate them,

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:49.800
as do three quarters of the plants
we grow for food.

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:52.640
It's no exaggeration to say
that without insects

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:54.920
many of us would starve.

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:58.840
But many types of insects
around the world are in big trouble.

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:01.600
Although measuring
insect populations is complex,

00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:03.960
there are worrying signs.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:08.520
A major 2020 study estimated
that globally land-dwelling insects

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:12.160
are declining by about 9%
per decade.

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:15.440
A German study found
that the biomass of flying insects

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:19.360
on nature reserves fell
by an alarming 76%

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:21.960
between 1989 and 2016.

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:26.360
In the United States,
populations of the monarch butterfly

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:28.600
are down 80% this century.

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:34.520
Some species, such as the comma and
speckled wood butterflies in the UK

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:36.000
are bucking the trend,

00:01:36.040 --> 00:01:39.520
but the overall geographic range
of butterflies in the UK

00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:44.800
has contracted on average
by 42% since 1976.

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:47.840
Invasive species
also take their toll.

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:51.600
Rats ate the St Helena giant earwig
to extinction

00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:55.600
and have nearly exterminated
the giant weta of New Zealand.

00:01:55.640 --> 00:01:59.480
Light pollution also lures moths
to bash themselves to death

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:03.240
and disrupts the timing
of insect life cycles.

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:06.080
It also disorients some dung beetles
who navigate

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:08.240
using the light of the Milky Way.

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:14.440
On top of all this, insects now
have to cope with climate change.

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:17.800
Some adaptable insects,
such as mosquitoes, cockroaches

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:22.960
and house flies, will benefit from
warmer temperatures and more rain.

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:24.800
Most insects will suffer.

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:29.000
Bumblebees are disappearing
from the southern edges

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:30.160
of their range,

00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:34.640
overheating in their furry coats
as the climate warms.

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:36.800
Droughts, floods and forest fires

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:40.160
will ravage already depleted
insect populations.

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:47.040
In 1962, American biologist
Rachel Carson wrote Silent Spring,

00:02:47.080 --> 00:02:51.080
warning that we were doing
terrible damage to our planet.

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:54.200
She wrote: "Man is a part of nature,

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:55.960
"and his war against nature

00:02:56.000 --> 00:02:58.480
"is inevitably
a war against himself."

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:02.360
But what she witnessed
was just the beginning.

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:04.720
Since then,
insect-rich wildlife habitats

00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:07.440
have been destroyed on a vast scale.

00:03:07.480 --> 00:03:10.800
Soils have been degraded
and rivers choked with silt,

00:03:10.840 --> 00:03:13.720
polluted or drained dry.

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:17.920
Farming, so reliant on insects
for pollination, is responsible

00:03:17.960 --> 00:03:20.040
for a large part of their decline.

00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:23.760
An estimated four million tonnes
of pesticides are released

00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:26.760
into the global environment
every year.

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:31.000
So what can we do if we want
to protect our insects?

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:34.360
The obvious place to start
is by rewilding our gardens

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:35.400
and balconies,

00:03:35.440 --> 00:03:38.120
inviting insects in
by planting wild flowers

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:39.800
and native shrubs,

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:43.680
reducing lawn-mowing and finding
alternatives to pesticides.

00:03:44.840 --> 00:03:47.920
But individual actions
won't be enough.

00:03:47.960 --> 00:03:52.080
Imagine green cities filled
with trees, vegetable gardens,

00:03:52.120 --> 00:03:54.040
ponds and unmowed verges,

00:03:54.080 --> 00:03:57.400
all free from pesticides
and buzzing with life.

00:03:57.440 --> 00:04:00.280
A movement towards
insect and nature-friendly

00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:02.240
sustainable farming is growing,

00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:04.440
but it needs much more support,

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:07.160
both from governments and consumers.

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:10.040
It's not quite too late.

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:13.160
Most threatened insect species
have not yet gone extinct

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:15.680
and could quickly recover
if protected.

00:04:17.080 --> 00:04:20.640
American biologist Paul Ehrlich
likened the loss of species

00:04:20.680 --> 00:04:24.480
to randomly popping out rivets
from the wing of an aeroplane.

00:04:24.520 --> 00:04:28.320
Remove one or two,
and the plane will probably be fine.

00:04:28.360 --> 00:04:31.040
Remove ten or 20 or 50,

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:34.040
and at some point, there will be
a catastrophic failure

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:35.960
and the plane will fall
from the sky.

00:04:37.240 --> 00:04:40.520
Insects are the rivets
that keep the planet running.

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:44.440
How many can be safely removed
from the plane before it crashes?

